If the image is overexposed, dull, or too dark, you can adjust the image's brightness and contrast using an image processing library such as OpenCV. Here is an example code snippet in C++ using OpenCV to adjust the brightness and contrast of an image:
```cpp
#include <opencv2/opencv.hpp>
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;
using namespace cv;

int main()
{
    // Load the image
    Mat image = imread("image.jpg", IMREAD_GRAYSCALE);

    // Adjust brightness and contrast
    Mat brightnessImage;
    cv::addWeighted(image, 1.5, Mat::zeros(image.size(), CV_8UC1), 0, Mat::zeros(image.size(), CV_8UC1), brightnessImage);
    cv::Mat contrastImage = cv::createLabFromInput(brightnessImage, 1, 0);
    cv::Mat invContrastImage;
    cv::split(contrastImage, invContrastImage, Mat::zeros(contrastImage.size(), CV_8UC1));
    cv::merge(invContrastImage, Mat::zeros(contrastImage.size(), CV_8UC1), brightnessImage);

    // Save the result
    imwrite("adjusted_image.jpg", brightnessImage);

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `cv::addWeighted()` function is used to adjust the brightness of the image. The `cv::createLabFromInput()` and `cv::split()` functions are used to adjust the contrast of the image. The `cv::merge()` function is used to combine the brightness and contrast adjusted images. Finally, the adjusted image is saved to a file using `imwrite()`. You can adjust the values of the parameters (such as 1.5 and 0) to achieve the desired brightness and contrast for your specific image.

